WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.370
Like all of Guillermo del Toro's films, "Pinocchio"
is complex, deeply contemplative and far more

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:07.292
than it seems.

00:00:07.292 --> 00:00:11.073
Here's what this master of the macabre has
to say about the classic tale.

00:00:11.073 --> 00:00:12.321
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:12.494 --> 00:00:16.920
The historical setting of "Guillermo del Toro's
Pinocchio" immediately lets you know that

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.789
the film will have a more serious focus than
past versions of the story.

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:24.689
The beginning of the movie takes place during
World War I, and as warplanes are making their

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:28.350
way back to base, they drop bombs just to
lighten their load.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:32.509
One of those bombs lands on Geppetto's church
and kills his son Carlo.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:36.090
From there the film's antiwar message becomes
even more explicit.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:39.630
By the time Geppetto creates Pinocchio, many
years have passed.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:44.890
World War I has come to a close, but Benito
Mussolini has come to power in Italy and fascists

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:49.540
dominate almost every aspect of daily life
in Geppetto's town and the surrounding areas.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:53.930
Geppetto, like many of the other Italian citizens,
does his best to play along with what the

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:57.760
fascists want to avoid any violence at their
hands.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.340
Pinocchio isn't so agreeable.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.460
He's a rambunctious young boy who likes to
do things his way.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.250
Just by being himself, Pinocchio shines a
light on just how wrong the fascist belief

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:07.390
system is.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.780
The primary antagonists of the film, the carnival
owner Count Volpe and the government representative

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:18.070
Podestà, are both fascists who want to crush
Pinocchio's spirit for the glory of the fatherland.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:23.079
It's Pinocchio's individualist streak, unlimited
optimism, and empathy for other people that

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:25.986
ultimately allow him to prevail over his foes.

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:30.619
In all its different forms, Pinocchio's story
is partly a coming-of-age tale about a boy

00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:34.290
learning how to be good and take responsibility
for his actions.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:38.460
In most versions of the tale, like Disney's
classic rendition from the 1940s, learning

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.905
to be good is synonymous with learning to
obey the people who are supposed to be in charge.

00:01:42.905 --> 00:01:47.141
Pinocchio usually learns that if he follows
what the adults in his life tell him, then

00:01:47.141 --> 00:01:49.460
things will generally work out for him.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:53.610
In Guillermo del Toro's version of the story,
however, most of the adults in Pinocchio's

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:59.149
life are fascists, espousing terrible rules
and life philosophies that only serve to devalue

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:00.000
other people.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.925
"Look at my boy Candlewick."

00:02:01.925 --> 00:02:03.090
"Yes, yes, yes…"

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.539
"A model fascist youth: proud, brave, virile!"

00:02:07.539 --> 00:02:12.160
Geppetto might not truly be a fascist himself,
but even Pinocchio's father can't serve as

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.723
a great example for the young boy because
he's willing to go along with what the fascists want.

00:02:16.723 --> 00:02:21.218
Unlike past versions of the story, Pinocchio
in this film doesn't need to learn how to obey.

00:02:21.218 --> 00:02:25.099
His disobedience is what sets him apart from
the other people in his life, and it's what

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:26.220
makes him a hero.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.626
For del Toro, Pinocchio's disobedience in
the film is the whole point.

00:02:29.626 --> 00:02:31.891
Speaking with the Washington Post, he said,

00:02:31.891 --> 00:02:34.800
"Disobedience now is more urgent than ever."

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.650
Pinocchio's real journey through the movie
is learning how to use that disobedience to

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:43.384
help others, like when he breaks Death's rules
and hourglass to save Geppetto's life.

00:02:43.551 --> 00:02:48.629
Pinocchio doesn't learn how to be obedient
in the film, but he does learn how to be good.

00:02:48.629 --> 00:02:52.000
At the beginning of the movie, Pinocchio breaks
rules to get what he wants.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.540
He leaves Geppetto's house to follow his father
to church.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.909
He skips out on going to school because he
wants to drink hot chocolate at the carnival.

00:02:57.909 --> 00:03:01.660
By the end of the movie, he's breaking rules
to save lives.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:02.970
What changed?

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:06.909
Pinocchio's journey away from home takes him
through plenty of hardships, but two moments,

00:03:06.909 --> 00:03:11.000
in particular, demonstrate how Pinocchio's
experiences cause him to change his attitude

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.030
in life.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:15.760
First, Pinocchio sees Count Volpe beating
and berating Spazzatura.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.830
He immediately steps in to defend the monkey,
and when that doesn't work, the two of them

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:23.060
conspire to sabotage the Count's performance
for Mussolini.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:27.080
Later Pinocchio is taken to a training camp
for fascist soldiers.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.170
There he comes to understand that his old
bully Candlewick was just being cruel because

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.420
that's what his father Podestà taught him
to do.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.760
Pinocchio and Candlewick become friends and
rebel against Podestà's rules.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.490
Throughout the film, Pinocchio suffers, but
he also witnesses others suffering of others

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.530
at the hands of the people in charge.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:49.110
All of that convinces him to change the way
he goes about disobeying and transforms him

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.340
into a true hero.

00:03:50.899 --> 00:03:54.170
Pinocchio isn't the only one who changes over
the course of the film.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:56.890
He also transforms the people who are closest
to him.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.680
Geppetto goes through the biggest change,
but Sebastian's transformation has the biggest

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:02.510
effect on the film's plot.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:06.439
After Geppetto loses his son Carlo, all his
joy for life vanishes.

00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:10.209
His work slows down to the point that even
when his town's church gets rebuilt, he never

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:14.640
finishes his new crucifix carving because
he spends the majority of his time drinking.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:21.237
The war ends in 1918, Benito Mussolini rises
to power in Italy in 1922, and Geppetto remains lost.

00:04:21.237 --> 00:04:25.510
The Wood Sprite gives Pinocchio life so he
can bring joy back into Geppetto's life.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:28.820
The two of them get off to a rough start,
but by the end of the movie, Geppetto has

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:31.760
found love and motivation all over again.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.400
He's willing to risk being eaten by a monstrous
sea creature just to see Pinocchio again.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.160
Sebastian begins as a self-centered writer
whose main interest in Pinocchio is that he

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.759
already has a home set up inside the wood
of the puppet's body.

00:04:43.759 --> 00:04:48.130
The Wood Sprite promises Sebastian one wish
if he teaches Pinocchio to be good, but things

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:49.930
work out in the opposite direction.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:53.970
Sebastian tries giving Pinocchio a lesson
or two, but by spending time with the boy,

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.240
Sebastian himself learns to be more selfless.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.460
He uses his wish, not for fame but to bring
Pinocchio back to life.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.419
"Well, gosh darn it.

00:05:02.791 --> 00:05:05.011
I wish him back to life!"

00:05:05.543 --> 00:05:09.710
There's another character in the movie who
goes through a drastic change thanks to Pinocchio,

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:12.870
and his transformation mirrors Pinocchio's
growth.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:18.497
Candlewick is the son of Podestà, a war-obsessed
fascist government official who lives in Pinocchio's town.

00:05:18.497 --> 00:05:22.039
When the boys first meet, Candlewick takes
a cue from his father and acts like a bully

00:05:22.039 --> 00:05:27.770
toward Pinocchio, going so far that he convinces
Pinocchio to stick his wooden legs in a fire.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.980
All that changes in the film's final act.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:34.190
Podestà ships Candlewick off to a special
military training camp for young boys, and

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:36.510
eventually Pinoccio finds himself there as
well.

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:41.259
Podestà is obsessed with the idea of using
Pinocchio as an immortal soldier, but neither

00:05:41.259 --> 00:05:44.380
of the children is genuinely interested in
fighting a war.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.830
At the training camp, Candlewick confesses
to Pinocchio that he just wants to make his

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.660
father proud of him, and Pinocchio comforts
him by telling him all fathers love their

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.340
sons, but fathers are still people and don't
always behave the way they should.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:06:00.699
In a training exercise, Pinocchio and Candlewick
come to a tie, and Podestà orders his son

00:06:00.699 --> 00:06:02.720
to shoot his new friend.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.180
Candlewick refuses, finally telling his father
that he doesn't need his approval.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:10.838
He and Pinocchio rebel against the camp's
leadership just as bombs begin to fall.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.600
Guillermo del Toro spends some time musing
on mortality in "Pinocchio."

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.350
Shortly after Count Volpe's carnival is introduced,
the Count and Geppetto get into a fight over

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:24.040
Pinocchio that ends when the puppet gets hit
by Podestà's car.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:27.940
To the humans, it appears as though Pinocchio
died, but he wakes up in a version of the

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:32.889
afterlife populated by skeletal rabbits and
a sphinx-like creature called Death who says

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:35.290
that the Wood Sprite is her sister.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:39.009
Death tells Pinocchio that the Wood Sprite
brought him to life because she doesn't care

00:06:39.009 --> 00:06:40.750
about following rules.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:44.569
Then Death explains the rules of Pinocchio's
new almost-afterlife.

00:06:44.569 --> 00:06:49.030
Every time he dies, he'll arrive in her world
and have to wait for larger and larger hourglasses

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:52.979
to run through their sand before he gets sent
back to the world of the living.

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:56.870
Near the end of the movie, Pinocchio dies
while Geppetto is drowning, and he breaks

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:02.150
the hourglass to get back more quickly, even
though it means he'll become truly mortal.

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:06.050
Pinocchio dies one final time in his efforts
to save Geppetto, but thanks to Sebastian's

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:10.080
wish and the Wood Sprite's magic, he gets
resurrected and has the chance to live out

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:11.830
his life with his family.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:16.120
Death may have warned Pinocchio that by breaking
the hourglass he'd gain mortality and become

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:19.800
"a real boy," but she also knew that her sister
would intervene.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:24.713
Death's warning was more about testing Pinocchio's
character than it was about limiting his lifespan.

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:28.780
When Sebastian enters the film, he begins
narrating different events.

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:32.440
He explains that when he first met Pinocchio,
he was a writer working on a book about his

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:37.000
life, and he tells the story of how he first
got involved with Geppetto, his puppet, and

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.050
the Wood Sprite.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:42.729
At the end of the film, Sebastian talks about
Pinocchio's long life with Geppetto and Spazzatura,

00:07:42.729 --> 00:07:46.139
who both eventually die as Pinocchio goes
on agelessly.

00:07:46.139 --> 00:07:50.650
In some instances of Sebastian's narration,
the camera shows him writing as he's speaking,

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:55.050
which make it all the more surprising when
Sebastian begins to narrate over his own death.

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:58.870
Just after seeing that Sebastian, like the
others, died, we catch up with him in the

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:02.430
afterlife, playing cards with the skeletal
rabbits who work for Death.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.340
The transition makes for a quick twist.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:08.760
It reveals that all this time, Sebastian wasn't
narrating from some grand book that he wrote

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:09.979
about his adventures.

00:08:09.979 --> 00:08:12.710
He gave up that dream to save Pinocchio's
life.

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:16.440
Instead, Sebastian has been telling his life
story to the disinterested rabbits over a

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:17.990
few hands of poker.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:22.639
The darkly humorous turn in the story is emblematic
of what sets Guillermo del Toro's "Pinocchio"

00:08:22.639 --> 00:08:24.904
apart from other takes on the story.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:29.659
Sadly, Pinocchio outlives Geppetto, Spazzatura,
and Sebastian.

00:08:29.659 --> 00:08:33.850
He's not a human being, and he never ages,
even as the people around him do.

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:38.029
The tragic part of his existence is something
that Death warns him about when he first visits

00:08:38.029 --> 00:08:39.099
her domain.

00:08:39.099 --> 00:08:43.289
Initially, Pinocchio is thrilled at the prospect
of being immortal, but Death tells him that

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:45.955
immortality is more of a curse than a blessing.

00:08:45.955 --> 00:08:53.282
"Life can bring suffering, and eternal life
can bring eternal suffering."

00:08:53.282 --> 00:08:57.519
When Pinocchio decides to break Death's hourglass
so he has enough time to save Geppetto from

00:08:57.519 --> 00:09:02.320
drowning in the sea, she tells him that not
following her rules will make him mortal again.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:07.811
Does the Wood Sprite's final spell, fulfilling
Sebastian's wish, undo Pinocchio's newfound mortality?

00:09:07.811 --> 00:09:09.245
Yes and no.

00:09:09.245 --> 00:09:12.910
Pinocchio is still on his "last life" like
Death told him he would be.

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:16.460
The Wood Sprite gives him one more chance
at that life, but she doesn't make Pinocchio

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:20.380
truly immortal again the way he was before
breaking the hourglass.

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:25.589
That said, Pinocchio is still a wooden puppet,
so he isn't likely to die from old age anytime soon.

00:09:25.589 --> 00:09:29.720
At the end of the film, Sebastian says that
he imagines Pinocchio didn't live forever,

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:34.393
but his friend still gets to have an extraordinarily
long life compared to normal humans.

00:09:34.814 --> 00:09:39.030
In the film, Death claims that Pinocchio's
"realness" is tied to his mortality.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:43.029
She says that human life is unique because
it is short, but even though Pinocchio gets

00:09:43.029 --> 00:09:46.910
to live for a very long time, he's still gone
through the transformation of becoming a real

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:48.790
boy by the end of the film.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:52.769
In more traditional versions of Pinocchio's
story, like Disney's classic film, his journey

00:09:52.769 --> 00:09:56.360
ends when he gets magically transformed into
a physical human being.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:59.560
That was never going to be the case in Guillermo
del Toro's version.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:01.880
Speaking with Vanity Fair del Toro revealed,

00:10:10.748 --> 00:10:15.149
Pinocchio's transformation from an inhuman
puppet to a real boy was a slow process that

00:10:15.149 --> 00:10:18.260
began when he first met Geppetto at the beginning
of the film.

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:22.810
In a way, every interaction he had with the
people he met further pushed him toward humanity,

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:26.820
but it was the moment that Pinocchio smashed
Death's hourglass that sealed his fate.

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:31.560
By being so full of love that he was willing
to sacrifice his own life, Pinocchio truly

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.902
embodied what it means to be human.

00:10:34.139 --> 00:10:39.000
Despite her massive impact on everything that
happens to Geppetto, Sebastian, and Pinocchio,

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.760
the movie doesn't reveal all that much about
the Wood Sprite.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:45.910
She first appears as a collection of glowing
orbs that seemingly take interest in Geppetto's

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:49.560
suffering as he chops down a tree while mourning
the death of his son Carlo.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:53.690
In his narration, Sebastian admits that he
knows very little about what the orbs are

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:56.460
or why they're suddenly flocking to Geppetto's
side.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:00.310
Later, when Pinocchio visits the afterlife
for the first time, Death tells him that the

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:02.029
Wood Sprite is her sister.

00:11:02.029 --> 00:11:06.120
The two of them share a resemblance, with
large wings that appear to have eyeballs sticking

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:07.290
out all over them.

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:11.060
But death gets more screen time and has much
more to say than her sister.

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:15.019
In her final appearance in the film, the Wood
Sprite grants Sebastian his wish and returns

00:11:15.019 --> 00:11:16.260
Pinocchio to life.

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:19.680
It seems like she might have known that things
would work out this way all along.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:27.839
"To save you, he became a real boy, and real
boys don't come back."

00:11:27.839 --> 00:11:32.110
However, it's unclear how much she and her
sister communicated and planned during the

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:33.389
events of the film.

00:11:33.389 --> 00:11:36.250
Ultimately, the Wood Sprite has little to
say or reveal.

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:40.662
She's simply a mysterious force of nature
that will never be fully understood.

